<h2>What Is Speed Score?</h2>
<p>Speed Score adjusts a 40 time for bodyweight, so a 230-pound back running 4.50 gets more credit than a 190-pound back running the same time. The formula is <strong>(weight × 200) ÷ (40 time⁴)</strong>. A score around <strong>100</strong> is typical for an NFL running back prospect, and <strong>110 or higher</strong> is excellent. It's most useful for running backs, tight ends and edge rushers, where size and speed both matter.</p>
<h2>How to Lower Your 40 Time</h2>
<p>Most of a 40 is won in the first 10 yards. Focus on <strong>start mechanics</strong> (stance, first two steps, shin angles), <strong>acceleration work</strong> like sled pushes and resisted sprints, and <strong>lower-body strength</strong> through squats, trap bar deadlifts and hip thrusts. Our <a href="/how-to-run-faster-40-yard-dash">complete guide to running a faster 40</a> lays out the full program. A focused 8–12 week block typically takes 0.1 to 0.3 seconds off.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is a good 40-yard dash time?</div>
<div class="faq-a">It depends on position and level. At the NFL combine, the average is about 4.47 to 4.53 seconds for cornerbacks, receivers, running backs and safeties, about 4.7 for linebackers, tight ends and quarterbacks, and 5.0 to 5.15 for linemen. For high school varsity skill players, 4.70 to 4.85 is average and under 4.50 is elite.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How do you convert a hand-timed 40 to electronic?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Add about 0.24 seconds. Hand-timed 40s are typically 0.2 to 0.3 seconds faster than electronic times because of human reaction time at the start and finish. A hand-timed 4.50 is roughly a 4.74 electronic.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How do you convert a 40-yard dash time to mph?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Divide 40 yards (36.58 meters) by your time to get meters per second, then multiply by 2.237. A 4.50 second 40 averages about 18.2 mph. Top speed during the run is higher than the average because the first 10 yards are spent accelerating.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is Speed Score in football?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Speed Score combines 40 time and bodyweight using the formula (weight × 200) ÷ (40 time to the fourth power). It rewards bigger players who run fast. A score near 100 is typical for an NFL running back prospect and 110 or higher is excellent.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is the fastest 40-yard dash at the NFL combine?</div>
<div class="faq-a">The official combine record is 4.21 seconds, run by Xavier Worthy in 2024, which broke John Ross's 4.22 from 2017. Times under 4.30 are rare even among NFL cornerbacks and receivers.</div>
</div>
